# Example environment variables for local development.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` at the repo root (that is the file the scripts below
# load, via process.loadEnvFile — not `.env.local`). `.env` is gitignored, so
# never commit real secrets.
#
# Every variable here is OPTIONAL — the repo builds and runs with none of them
# set. Each is an override of a default that already lives in committed config.
# They are left commented out on purpose: an empty `KEY=` copied into `.env`
# sets the variable to an empty string, which the `??` defaulting below does NOT
# treat as "unset" — it would break `registry:sync` and the apps/web build.
# Uncomment and set a value only when you actually need to override the default.

# --- Registry URLs -----------------------------------------------------------
# The shadcn registry has two URLs, both stored in registry.config.json (the
# committed source of truth). These vars only override that config locally.
# See AGENTS.md §7 "The registry's two URLs — location vs origin".
# Both are also listed in turbo.json `globalEnv` (with NODE_ENV) so Turbo keys
# its build cache on them.

# Public URL consumers install from — the registry project's own domain.
# Edit this, then run `npm run registry:sync` to propagate it into
# registry.config.json, the docs, and the generated registry JSON.
# Consumed by scripts/sync-registry-location.mjs. Default: `.location` in
# registry.config.json. In CI this is the `REGISTRY_LOCATION` repo variable.
# REGISTRY_LOCATION=https://registry.design.nsw.gov.au

# Upstream that the apps/web legacy `/registry` rewrite proxies TO — the
# registry's own domain. MUST NOT be a path on the web app's domain, or the
# proxy loops.
# Consumed by apps/web/next.config.mjs. Default: `.origin` in registry.config.json.
# REGISTRY_ORIGIN=https://registry.design.nsw.gov.au

# --- Zones on ui.design.nsw.gov.au --------------------------------------------
# The web app serves /infographics and /storybook by rewriting them to those
# projects' own domains (apps/web/zones.mjs, AGENTS.md §7). Point a zone at a
# local server to work on it behind the web app, e.g. the infographics app's
# `npm run dev -w infographics`. Unlike the registry vars above, an empty value
# falls back to the default. Both are in turbo.json `globalEnv`.
# INFOGRAPHICS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001
# STORYBOOK_ORIGIN=http://localhost:6006

# --- Chromatic ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Project token for the Chromatic visual-regression service. SECRET — do not
# commit the real value. In CI it is the `CHROMATIC_PROJECT_TOKEN` repo secret,
# consumed by the chromaui/action step in .github/workflows/chromatic.yml. No
# repo script runs Chromatic locally; set this only if you publish Storybook to
# Chromatic yourself (e.g. `npx chromatic` from apps/storybook).
# CHROMATIC_PROJECT_TOKEN=chpt_0000000000000000000000000000000000000

# NODE_ENV is intentionally not listed: it is managed by the tooling (Next,
# Vitest, tsup) and appears in turbo.json `globalEnv` only for cache keying.
# Setting it by hand in a dotenv file is an anti-pattern — leave it to the tools.

# --- Sign-in (apps/web and apps/infographics) ---------------------------------
# Until launch both Next apps sit behind sign-in (packages/auth, each app's
# proxy.ts). They sign in against the one database and secret every NSW Design
# site shares (nswds-design's), and in production the session cookie is scoped
# to design.nsw.gov.au, so one sign-in covers design., ui. and email. Each app's
# next.config loads this root .env. The database's schema and migrations are
# nswds-design's (npm run db:migrate there); this repo runs none.
# DATABASE_URL, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET and BETTER_AUTH_URL are deploy requirements.
# The apps still build without them (a placeholder connection string keeps
# `next build` from crashing), but a deployment missing any of them is
# unusable: the gate fails closed, so every page redirects to sign-in and
# sign-in itself refuses (no database, Better Auth's default secret, or an
# untrusted origin). SIGNUP_ALLOWED_EMAILS only governs who may register:
# unset or empty, nobody can create an account, but existing accounts still
# sign in. In Vercel set them on the web and infographics projects alike — the
# same values on both.

# Neon Postgres connection string — from the Neon dashboard, or `vercel env pull`
# if you provision Neon through the Vercel Marketplace. Read by Better Auth at
# runtime. Keep the ?sslmode=require suffix.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxxx-xxxx.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# Secret Better Auth signs session tokens/cookies with. The SAME value on every
# app that shares the database, or a session from one app is invalid on the
# others. Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-a-32-byte-random-secret

# The app's origin (scheme + host, NO path), used for callbacks and the
# trusted-origin check. In development any localhost port is trusted, so the
# value does not gate sign-in locally. In PRODUCTION it MUST be the origin the
# app is reached on, or requests are rejected with "Invalid origin": for both
# projects that is https://ui.design.nsw.gov.au, the domain the zones are
# served through.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Comma- or whitespace-separated list of email addresses allowed to register.
# Self-service sign-up is restricted to these (matched case-insensitively);
# anyone else is rejected with a 403. Fail-closed — unset or empty, NOBODY can
# register — so set it before expecting sign-up to work.
# Registration is one-shot per address (there is no email verification): the
# first person to sign up with an allowlisted address owns it, so claim each
# address before the site is shared.
# SIGNUP_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com, teammate@example.com

# Lifts the sign-in gate for a browser suite that runs a built app with no
# database, e.g. `NSWDS_AUTH_GATE=off next start`. Honoured on localhost only
# (packages/auth/src/proxy.ts), so setting it in Vercel changes nothing.
# NSWDS_AUTH_GATE=off

# Analytics (packages/analytics). Both are team shared variables in Vercel.
#
# The PostHog project token, on Preview and Production. PostHog starts only on a production
# deployment (NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_ENV=production, which Vercel sets), so a local run stays off
# whatever this holds; set both to try it against the real project.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=phc_0000000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# The Google Tag Manager container the NSW Design sites share, on Production only. Unset renders no
# tag, which is right for local runs and previews.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=GTM-XXXXXXX
